{"results":[{"url":"https://lawfold.com/trump-lawsuit/","final_url":"https://lawfold.com/trump-lawsuit/","title":"Trump Lawsuit: All Major Cases and Key Updates 2026","description":"From the $10B IRS lawsuit to 530 state challenges, get clear answers on every major Trump lawsuit, settlement news, and what it means for you in 2026.","language":"en","text":"Latest Update: As of July 10, 2026, two of this guide’s central storylines have moved. On June 30, 2026, the Supreme Court ruled 6-3 in Trump v. Barbara that Trump’s executive order ending birthright citizenship violates the 14th Amendment, upholding automatic citizenship for children born on U.S. soil. Trump announced on July 8, 2026 that he will ask the Court for a rehearing, an outcome legal experts call a long shot since the Court hasn’t granted one in a merits case since 1965. Separately, Trump’s $10 billion IRS lawsuit, which he settled and voluntarily dismissed in May 2026 in exchange for a $1.776 billion “Anti-Weaponization Fund,” was reopened by a federal judge on July 1, 2026 after 35 former federal judges challenged the settlement as collusive.\n\nLast updated: July 2026\n\nThe Trump lawsuit story in 2026 is unlike anything in American legal history. Over 530 lawsuits were filed against the Trump administration in its first year alone. That number has kept climbing.\n\nFrom states suing over immigration orders to Trump personally suing his own government for $10 billion, the legal battles touch almost every corner of American life. Tariffs, DOGE cuts, birthright citizenship, federal funding, and worker rights are all in play.\n\nRelated: Trump Voting Rights Lawsuits 2026: All Key Cases Explained\n\nThis guide breaks down every major category. Plain English. No fluff. Just the facts you need to understand what’s happening and what it means for you.\n\n## What Is a Trump Lawsuit?\n\nA Trump lawsuit refers to any legal action filed either against Donald Trump, against his administration’s policies, or by Trump himself against other parties. In 2025 and 2026, these cases span two very different directions.\n\nThe first direction: hundreds of lawsuits filed by states, civil rights groups, labor unions, and individuals challenging the Trump administration’s executive orders and policy actions.\n\nThe second direction: lawsuits filed by Trump personally, most notably a $10 billion suit against his own federal government over leaked tax returns.\n\n| Type | Who Filed | What It Challenges |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Against Trump Admin | States, nonprofits, unions | Executive orders, funding cuts, deportations |\n| By Trump | Trump personally | IRS leak, DOJ investigations |\n| Business/historical | Private parties | Contracts, defamation, property |\n\nBoth directions involve billions of dollars and Supreme Court-level constitutional questions. Both affect ordinary Americans.\n\n## How Many Lawsuits Against Trump Have Been Filed?\n\nLegal analysts estimate that 20 to 30 of the 530 current lawsuits against the Trump administration are likely to reach the Supreme Court.\n\nAs of January 3, 2026, the Associated Press was tracking 358 cases, grouping them into 14 categories including immigration, DOGE cuts, and civil liberties.\n\nPresident Donald Trump was facing roughly 400 lawsuits from his executive orders, far more than had been filed at the same point during his first term.\n\n| Time Period | Lawsuits Filed | Key Categories |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| First 100 days (2025) | 100+ | Immigration, funding, DOGE |\n| By end of 2025 | 530+ | All policy areas |\n| As of early 2026 | 600+ | Tariffs, birthright citizenship, climate |\n\nNo president in history has faced this volume of legal challenges in such a short period. That’s not a partisan opinion. It’s the documented conclusion of organizations tracking the cases across the political spectrum.\n\n## Trump Administration Lawsuits 2025 and 2026: The Big Picture\n\nTrump signed more executive orders in 2025 than during all four years of his first presidency combined, and the pace of those actions triggered a wave of lawsuits seeking to block or pause many of the orders.\n\nThe cases cluster into clear policy areas: immigration enforcement, DOGE-led government cuts, tariff authority, civil rights protections, and federal funding freezes.\n\nBy mid-July 2025, a Washington Post analysis found the administration defied judges and courts in roughly one third of all cases against it, with legal experts describing that rate as unprecedented for any presidential administration.\n\n| Policy Area | Number of Cases (approx.) | Current Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Immigration | 100+ | Mixed rulings, SCOTUS pending |\n| DOGE / Workforce | 50+ | Several injunctions issued |\n| Tariffs | 8 principal cases | SCOTUS ruled against tariffs |\n| Civil rights | 30+ | Multiple injunctions in force |\n| Federal funding | 25+ | Split rulings |\n\nThe sheer breadth of these cases means virtually every American is touched by at least one of them.\n\nKey Takeaway: The Trump administration faces more lawsuits in its second term than any presidency in modern history, covering policy areas from immigration to climate to federal workers’ rights.\n\n## Lawsuits Against Trump Executive Orders\n\nLawsuits have challenged Trump’s most sweeping executive orders, ranging from a ban on birthright citizenship and transgender service members in the military to the legality of DOGE-led government cuts and the president’s use of emergency powers.\n\nThe legal standard courts use is the Administrative Procedure Act (APA). It requires federal agencies to follow proper rulemaking procedures. Many Trump executive orders skipped those steps.\n\nThink of the APA like a building permit process. You can’t just start demolishing a structure because you want to. The law requires you to file paperwork, give notice, and follow procedure. Courts have repeatedly found that Trump’s team skipped that process.\n\n| Executive Order | Challenge Based On | Court Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Birthright citizenship end | 14th Amendment | Blocked nationwide |\n| Federal grant freeze | APA + separation of powers | Blocked, reversed on appeal |\n| Transgender military ban | Equal protection | Allowed by SCOTUS |\n| DEI terminations | Civil Rights Act, Title VI | Injunctions issued |\n\nSeveral of these cases are still working through appeals courts as of March 2026.\n\n## Trump’s $10 Billion IRS Lawsuit\n\nBefore January 29, 2026, no sitting president had ever sued his own government in court. Trump changed that.\n\nTrump filed a $10 billion lawsuit against the Department of Treasury and IRS over a 2019 leak of his tax returns by a contractor, alleging the leak caused “irreparable harm” to his family and businesses.\n\nTreasury Secretary Scott Bessent confirmed in a Senate Banking Committee hearing that if Trump wins the lawsuit, the payout would come from the Treasury, meaning American taxpayers would foot the bill.\n\n| Detail | Info |\n| --- | --- |\n| Amount sought | $10 billion |\n| Filed | January 29, 2026 |\n| Court | U.S. District Court, Southern District of Florida |\n| Defendant | IRS and Treasury Department |\n| Who pays if Trump wins | U.S. taxpayers |\n\nExperts agree that the government will seek to bring the complaint to a speedy resolution, though a court date has not yet been set.\n\n## The Trump Tariff Lawsuit\n\nThe Supreme Court decided the legality of Trump declaring a national emergency to impose sweeping tariffs on foreign goods without congressional approval, in what Trump himself called the “most important case ever,” known as Learning Resources v. Trump.\n\nIn a 6-3 ruling, the justices ruled against most of President Donald Trump’s sweeping tariffs, the cornerstone of his economic agenda. This is the most significant Supreme Court loss of Trump’s second term.\n\nThe ruling does not make clear if the administration must refund more than $100 billion in tariff payments already collected. That question is still unresolved.\n\n| Detail | Info |\n| --- | --- |\n| Case name | Learning Resources v. Trump |\n| Legal basis challenged | IEEPA (International Emergency Economic Powers Act) |\n| SCOTUS ruling | 6-3 against most tariffs |\n| Tariffs collected | $100B+ |\n| Refund status | Unclear as of March 2026 |\n\nIf refunds are eventually ordered, businesses and importers who paid those tariffs could be in line for reimbursement.\n\nKey Takeaway: The Supreme Court struck down most of Trump’s tariffs in a 6-3 ruling, but whether over $100 billion in already-collected tariff payments must be refunded remains an open legal question.\n\n## Multistate Lawsuits Against Trump\n\nAs of May 25, 2025, 24 multistate lawsuits had been filed against the second Trump administration. State attorneys general from both coasts have been driving these cases.\n\nTwenty-two states and the District of Columbia, led by New York Attorney General Letitia James, filed a lawsuit against Trump on January 28, 2025, challenging an OMB directive that paused billions in federal grants and financial assistance.\n\nIn March 2026, a group of 23 states filed a legal petition challenging Trump’s revocation of the EPA’s climate endangerment finding, with the challenge led by California and New York, filed in the U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia.\n\n| State Coalition | Target | Date |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 22 states + D.C. | Federal grant freeze | January 28, 2025 |\n| 8 states | Education funding cuts | Early 2025 |\n| 23 states | EPA climate finding repeal | March 19, 2026 |\n| Multiple states | USDA funding freeze | March 23, 2026 |\n\nThese coalitions represent the broadest legal opposition to any administration in American history.\n\n## Trump Immigration Lawsuit Cases\n\nImmigration is the single largest category of Trump litigation. According to Politico, there were “more than 100 lawsuits and 50 restraining orders from dozens of federal judges” related to the removal of F-1 foreign student visa registrations alone, before the government reversed its decision.\n\nSince Trump’s 2025 inauguration, data shows 393,000 immigration arrests, 68,289 people held in detention centers, and 32 people who died in ICE custody during 2025.\n\nAt least 3,800 children under age 18 were booked into ICE detention centers, with over 500 under age five, and at least 1,000 held longer than 30 days, which violates a court-ordered limit on child detention.\n\n| Immigration Action | Lawsuit Status | Key Ruling |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Birthright citizenship end | Blocked by multiple courts | SCOTUS hearing in 2026 |\n| Mass deportations (Alien Enemies Act) | Challenged | Partially blocked |\n| F-1 student visa removals | Reversed after 100+ lawsuits | Registrations restored |\n| Mandatory detention policy | Challenged | Ongoing |\n\nThe immigration cases alone could reshape who is considered a U.S. citizen and who can be deported.\n\n## The Trump DOGE Lawsuit\n\nDOGE (Department of Government Efficiency), led by Elon Musk, triggered dozens of lawsuits almost immediately after taking action in early 2025.\n\nAFGE led a coalition of unions and other plaintiffs challenging Executive Order 14210, which directed agencies to eliminate or consolidate programs and initiate large-scale reductions in force. HHS planned to cut 10,000+ positions, DOE proposed 50%+ workforce cuts, and CDC faced 93% cuts to some offices.\n\nTrump identified Elon Musk as leading DOGE, though Musk did not hold the formal title of DOGE Administrator. Musk began cost-cutting measures including layoffs, shutting down departments and agencies, and removing programs.\n\n| Agency | Proposed Cuts | Legal Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| HHS | 10,000+ jobs | Injunction issued, stayed by SCOTUS |\n| DOE | 50%+ workforce | Blocked initially |\n| CDC | 93% of some offices | Blocked initially |\n| SBA, GSA, State, Education | Mass RIF notices | Rescinded after court order |\n\nKey Takeaway: DOGE-related lawsuits blocked mass federal layoffs at dozens of agencies, though many of those injunctions were later stayed or overturned on appeal.\n\n## Trump Birthright Citizenship Lawsuit\n\nFour states, led by Washington Attorney General Nick Brown, argued that Trump’s executive order ending birthright citizenship violated the Fourteenth Amendment, which guarantees citizenship to all persons born in the United States and subject to its jurisdiction.\n\nOpponents argue the effort is unconstitutional and would threaten an estimated 150,000 children born annually in the U.S. to parents who are noncitizens, and an estimated 4.4 million American-born children under 18 living with an undocumented immigrant parent.\n\nThe Supreme Court has agreed to decide whether Trump’s birthright citizenship order can stand, with oral arguments in 2026 and a ruling expected by late June.\n\n| Detail | Info |\n| --- | --- |\n| Executive order signed | January 20, 2025 |\n| Constitutional challenge | 14th Amendment |\n| Children potentially affected | 150,000+ born annually |\n| SCOTUS hearing | Early 2026 |\n| Ruling expected | By June 2026 |\n\nThis could be the single most constitutionally significant case of Trump’s second term. If the court upholds the order, it would overturn over 125 years of settled law.\n\n## Trump Lawsuit at the Supreme Court in 2026\n\nNever before has the Supreme Court been asked to rule on the legality of so many presidential actions in such a short period. During the first year of this Trump presidency, the court overwhelmingly sided with the Trump administration.\n\nBut 2026 has already changed that. The tariff ruling was a major loss.\n\nSCOTUS is also deciding the legality of Trump’s use of declared emergencies to enact his economic agenda, whether he can fire a member of the Federal Reserve, and the constitutionality of his birthright citizenship executive order.\n\n| Case | Issue | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Learning Resources v. Trump | Tariff authority under IEEPA | Ruled 6-3 against Trump |\n| Trump v. Barbara | Birthright citizenship | Ruling expected by June 2026 |\n| Federal Reserve removal | Presidential removal power | Pending |\n| Trump v. CASA | Scope of nationwide injunctions | Ruled for Trump in 2025 |\n\nThis year, the court will decide cases about the president’s power to remove agency heads, the legality of Trump’s tariffs, and the constitutionality of his executive order on birthright citizenship.\n\n## Trump Lawsuit Settlement: What’s Possible\n\nSettlement talk in Trump-related litigation takes two very different forms.\n\nThe first: states and advocacy groups settling with the administration after injunctions force policy reversals. On February 9, 2026, LDF reached a settlement in NAACP v. U.S. Department of Education, in which the Education Department agreed it would no longer rely on a controversial “Dear Colleague Letter.”\n\nThe second: Trump’s own claim for settlement from the government in his $10B IRS suit.\n\nTrump appeared to suggest that if there is a settlement and it is a substantial amount, “nobody will care because the money would go to charity.”\n\n| Settlement Type | Example | Amount |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Policy reversal settlement | NAACP v. DOE | No financial payout |\n| Injunction-based agreement | AFGE union CBAs reinstated | Workers restored |\n| Trump’s personal claim | IRS tax leak case | Up to $10 billion sought |\n\nLegal settlements in public interest cases rarely involve cash. They usually mean the government changes its behavior.\n\n## Lawsuit Against Trump: Who Stands to Get a Payout?\n\nMost lawsuits against the Trump administration don’t produce cash settlements for individuals. They produce policy changes and injunctions.\n\nBut some cases do have financial stakes. A ruling against the Trump tariffs could force the government to refund more than $100 billion in tariffs already collected, though it’s unclear whether refunds are required.\n\nDemocratic lawmakers introduced the Prevent Presidential Profiteering Act, which would impose a 100% tax on any civil judgment or settlement paid by the United States to a sitting president or the president’s immediate family if the legal action was filed while in office.\n\n| Case Type | Who Benefits | Estimated Amount |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Tariff refunds | Businesses that paid tariffs | Up to $100B+ (disputed) |\n| Federal funding cases | States and institutions | Billions in restored grants |\n| Worker reinstatement cases | Federal employees | Jobs and back pay |\n| IRS lawsuit (Trump’s claim) | Trump personally | Up to $10B (contested) |\n\nFor most ordinary Americans, the benefit of these lawsuits is restored services, reinstated workers, or reversed policies, not direct checks.\n\nKey Takeaway: Most Trump-related lawsuit settlements result in policy reversals rather than cash payouts to individuals, though tariff litigation could eventually produce refunds for businesses.\n\n## Can You Join a Lawsuit Against Trump?\n\nJoining a lawsuit against the Trump administration depends entirely on what the lawsuit covers and how you were affected.\n\nIndividual Americans can typically participate through three paths.\n\n* Class actions: If a policy harmed a defined group you belong to (e.g., federal workers fired without cause), a class action attorney can add you to the case.\n* State AG lawsuits: If your state’s attorney general filed suit, you benefit automatically if they win. You don’t need to do anything.\n* Civil rights organizations: Groups like the ACLU and NAACP LDF file on behalf of affected individuals. Contact them directly if a policy harmed you.\n\n| Path | Who Qualifies | Action Required |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Class action | Specific affected groups | Contact a class action attorney |\n| State AG lawsuits | Residents of participating states | None, automatic benefit |\n| Nonprofit-led cases | Civil rights violations | Contact the filing organization |\n| Federal worker cases | Former/current federal employees | Join through union or AFGE |\n\nThe most direct route for most people: identify your state’s attorney general office and see which suits they have filed in your interest.\n\n## Trump Lawsuit Update 2026: What’s Happening Right Now\n\nAs of March 2026, the legal landscape is moving fast.\n\nMichigan and Maryland attorneys general filed lawsuits against the Trump administration on March 23, 2026, challenging the administration’s holding of billions in critical USDA funding.\n\nThe Supreme Court ruled 6-3 against Trump’s tariffs in Learning Resources v. Trump, marking the most significant court loss of his second term.\n\nOn March 7, 2026, Judge Lamberth granted summary judgment ruling that a Trump appointee’s tenure as acting CEO of USAGM was unlawful and voided her actions, including mass layoffs.\n\n| Recent Update | Date | Outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SCOTUS rules against Trump tariffs | 2026 | 6-3 loss for Trump |\n| USDA funding lawsuits filed | March 23, 2026 | Ongoing |\n| USAGM acting CEO tenure ruled unlawful | March 7, 2026 | Layoffs voided |\n| Climate endangerment finding challenge | March 19, 2026 | 23 states, ongoing |\n\nNew lawsuits are being filed almost weekly. The pace of litigation shows no sign of slowing before the 2026 midterm elections.\n\n## Lawsuit Trump: A Brief History of Trump’s Personal Legal Record\n\nBefore becoming president a second time, Trump filed a claim against the Department of Justice asking for $230 million to compensate him for the FBI investigation that found official and classified documents at Mar-a-Lago. In 2026, he filed a lawsuit against his own Department of the Treasury, asking for $10 billion in compensation for a leak of his tax returns during his first term.\n\nTrump’s personal legal history goes back decades. He has been both an aggressive plaintiff and a frequent defendant.\n\nFor Trump, filing lawsuits, including those that are frivolous or where he has little chance of success, has long been standard operating procedure, a way of communicating displeasure.\n\n| Case Category | Examples | Outcome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Business disputes | USFL antitrust case | Won $3 verdict; USFL folded |\n| Defamation suits | Roffman settlement, CNN lawsuit | Mixed; CNN case dismissed |\n| Government claims | $230M DOJ claim, $10B IRS suit | Ongoing or pending |\n| Property disputes | Palm Beach flagpole | $100K charity donation settlement |\n\nThe pattern is consistent: Trump uses litigation as a tool, not just a last resort.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### How many lawsuits have been filed against Trump?\n\nOver 530 lawsuits were filed against the Trump administration in 2025 alone, far exceeding any prior president in the same timeframe. As of early 2026, that number has grown further, with new suits filed almost weekly. Multiple organizations including Just Security, Lawfare, and the Associated Press track these cases in real time.\n\n### What is Trump’s $10 billion IRS lawsuit about?\n\nTrump filed a $10 billion lawsuit against the IRS and Treasury Department over the 2019 leak of his tax returns by an IRS contractor, alleging the leak caused irreparable harm to his family and businesses. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent confirmed that if Trump wins, U.S. taxpayers would pay the bill. Legal experts say the case faces significant obstacles, including a potential statute of limitations problem and a glaring conflict of interest.\n\n### Can I join a lawsuit against the Trump administration?\n\nMost people benefit from state attorney general lawsuits automatically, with no action required. If you are a federal worker, a student, or a civil rights victim, contact organizations like the ACLU, NAACP LDF, or AFGE to find active cases that may apply to you. For business owners affected by tariffs, consult a trade attorney about pending cases in the Court of International Trade.\n\n### What did the Supreme Court rule on Trump’s tariff lawsuit?\n\nIn a 6-3 ruling, the justices ruled against most of President Trump’s sweeping tariffs, the cornerstone of his economic agenda. A ruling against the tariffs could force the government to refund more than $100 billion in tariff payments already collected, though that question remains unresolved. The case, Learning Resources v. Trump, is the most significant court loss of Trump’s second term so far.\n\n### What is the latest update on Trump lawsuits in 2026?\n\nAs of March 23, 2026, Michigan and Maryland filed new lawsuits challenging the Trump administration’s freezing of critical USDA funding. On March 19, 2026, 23 states filed a legal petition challenging Trump’s revocation of the EPA’s climate endangerment finding. The Supreme Court is also expected to rule on birthright citizenship by June 2026, a decision that could affect millions of American families.\n\nThe legal battles surrounding the Trump administration are the most consequential since Watergate. They touch federal workers, immigrants, students, businesses, and taxpayers.\n\nStay informed. If a lawsuit covers a policy that directly affected you, don’t wait to find out what you’re entitled to.\n\nCheck your state’s attorney general website. Contact civil rights organizations. And keep following these cases as they move toward the Supreme Court in the months ahead.","author":"Owen Parker","published_date":"2026-03-24T05:34:33+05:00","latency_ms":1636.3043370001833,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}